# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Rest In Prompts — environment variables
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. NEVER commit real secrets.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Database driver selection.
#
#   DATABASE_URL set    -> node-postgres against a hosted/managed Postgres
#   DATABASE_URL unset  -> PGlite, an embedded Postgres persisted to ./.pglite
#
# Leave this commented out for local development: the app boots with zero
# external services. REQUIRED in production — PGlite writes to disk, so a
# serverless deployment without DATABASE_URL fails fast on first DB access.
#
# On Vercel, use Neon's *pooled* connection string (hostname contains
# `-pooler`). Use the direct one only for `npm run db:migrate`.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/rest_in_prompts

# Where PGlite persists its data when DATABASE_URL is unset.
# Relative paths resolve from the repo root. Optional.
# PGLITE_DATA_DIR=./.pglite

# TLS for the node-postgres driver. Defaults to on *with certificate
# verification*, which is what Neon / Supabase / RDS all support. Set to
# "false" only for a local, trusted Postgres reached over a private network.
# DATABASE_SSL=true

# Connection pool size per process. Defaults to 1, which is what a serverless
# deployment wants: every function instance has its own pool, so anything
# larger multiplies against the instance count and exhausts Neon's free-tier
# connection limit. Raise it only for a long-lived server.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=1

# How long an idle pooled connection is kept before being released.
# Defaults to 10000 ms. Short on purpose, for the same reason as above.
# DATABASE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Escape hatch: allow PGlite while NODE_ENV=production. Only for smoke-testing
# a local `npm run build && npm start`. Never set this on a deployment.
# PGLITE_ALLOW_PRODUCTION=true

# ── Billing (T6 / ZCZ-7) — Stripe TEST MODE ONLY ─────────────────────────────
#
# Leave every variable below unset and the app runs in "simulated" billing
# mode: the pricing page's upgrade button flips the account to pro directly,
# labels the change as a local simulation, and touches no external service.
# That is the intended default — the whole free → pro → export → API-key chain
# is demonstrable with no third-party account at all.
#
# Setting STRIPE_SECRET_KEY switches to "stripe_test" mode. The app REFUSES a
# live key (`sk_live_…`): see `src/lib/billing/config.ts`. This project never
# captures a real payment.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID_PRO=price_...            # a test-mode recurring price
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...   # reserved; unused today

# The endpoint secret for POST /api/billing/webhook. Honoured in BOTH modes:
# with a secret set but no Stripe key, the real signature-verification and
# plan-upgrade code can be exercised by posting a correctly signed synthetic
# event — which is how `npm run verify:saas` tests the webhook leg without a
# Stripe account. Any `whsec_…`-shaped string works for that.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# Forces simulated mode even when Stripe keys are present.
# BILLING_SIMULATION=true

# Absolute origin used for Stripe's success/cancel redirects. On Vercel this
# falls back to VERCEL_URL; locally it falls back to http://127.0.0.1:3000.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://<project>.vercel.app

# ── Email subscription (T10 / ZCZ-11) ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There are NO variables for this. By product decision (2026-11-03) the site
# collects subscriptions and sends nothing: `src/lib/mailer.ts` has one driver,
# `log`, which writes the message to the server log and reports it as NOT
# delivered. There is no MAIL_PROVIDER / MAIL_FROM / API key to set, and setting
# one would do nothing — picking a provider needs an authorization card from the
# user and a driver that does not exist yet.
#
# Consequences worth knowing before you deploy:
#
#  - the subscribe form says "已登记，确认邮件会在邮件服务接通后发出" and never
#    claims a mail was sent;
#  - subscriptions stay `pending` indefinitely. That is the correct state, not a
#    backlog: `confirmed` means the person clicked a link in a mail, and no mail
#    has been sent. /admin/subscribers says so, and offers no way to override it.
#
# The confirm / unsubscribe routes are live and testable: the logged message
# contains both links. Their origin comes from NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL (above), then
# VERCEL_URL, then the request's Host header — set it on a deployment so the
# links never depend on an incoming header.

# Daily ingestion (UTC 00:00 / Asia-Shanghai 08:00).
# Set a random server-side secret before enabling the Vercel cron endpoint.
# An unset CRON_SECRET rejects every request. Never use a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
# CRON_SECRET=
# INGESTION_DAILY_LIMIT=3
# INGESTION_BUDGET_MS=40000
